@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a string containing JSON. The output is valid JSON, indented by 4 spaces by default and syntax highlighted. Use the ``indent`` keyword argument to change the number of spaces, and ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+You can also log a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping. When printing with default options, lines longer than the console width are cropped. Pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` to keep long lines whole.
+
+The package also has a :func:`rich.print_json` function which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich"}')
+
+To pretty print a JSON file from the command line, run the json module with the path to the file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
